\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{Coefficient rows.} One row per predictor coefficient, on the
log-odds scale under the logit link. Under \code{exponentiate = TRUE} they
are proportional odds ratios (logit), or hazard ratios of the grouped
event under the cloglog link (see \code{exponentiate} below).

\strong{Thresholds.} The cut-points render as a \code{Thresholds} block, with B,
SE, CI and p like the predictor rows (\code{show_thresholds}). They are
reported on the log-odds scale and never exponentiated.

\strong{Non-proportional effects.} The terms of a \code{clm} fit given in
\verb{nominal =} (partial proportional odds) render as a \verb{Non-proportional effects} block, one coefficient per cut-point. The covariate effects of
\verb{scale =} render as a \verb{Scale effects} block (\code{show_components}).

\strong{AME.} Per category: the marginal effect of the predictor on each
\eqn{P(Y = k)}{P(Y = k)}, for \code{polr} and \code{clm} fits.

\strong{Fit statistics.} By default, \code{n}, McFadden's pseudo-R-squared and
\code{AIC}. See \emph{R-squared of glm and categorical models} in
\code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
}

\section{Arguments for this family}{

\describe{
\item{\code{show_thresholds}}{Logical. \code{TRUE} (default) displays the
cut-points as a \code{Thresholds} block of rows below the predictors,
carrying B, SE, CI and p like the predictor rows. \code{FALSE} collapses
them to a one-line note instead. Thresholds are reported on the
log-odds (B) scale and are never exponentiated. They follow the
\code{polr} convention, link(P(Y <= j)) = threshold - linear predictor:
an \code{rms::lrm} fit, which models P(Y >= j), shows its intercepts with
the sign reversed, named \code{"3|4"} (displayed \code{"3 | 4"}) rather than
\code{"y>=4"}. The rows are shown only when a coefficient column (\code{"b"}
or \code{"beta"}) is in \code{show_columns}; otherwise the cut-points go to
the note. Applies to \code{polr}, \code{clm}, \code{clmm},
\code{svyolr}, \code{glmmTMB} with the ordinal family, and \code{rms::lrm} with
more than two outcome levels.}
\item{\code{show_components}}{Logical. \code{TRUE} (default) displays the
\verb{scale = ~} effects of a \code{clm} fit as a \verb{Scale effects} block: the
covariate effects on the log standard deviation of the latent
response. They are never exponentiated: their exponential is a
ratio of latent standard deviations, not an odds ratio. \code{FALSE}
omits the block. The argument also governs the zero-inflation and
hurdle blocks of the count models (\link{table_regression_counts}).}
}

